\subsection{Comparative evaluation}
\label{sec:observer-comparison}

\subsubsection{Deterministic external and predecessor baselines}
The deterministic comparison includes the adaptive PII observer, OU--II, and an
adapted vertical-reference configuration of the time-varying-gain nonlinear
observer (TVG--NLO) of Bryne, Fossen, and Johansen~\cite{BryneFossenJohansen2014_TVG_NLO}.
The TVG--NLO configuration omits the horizontal GNSS and compass aiding of the
published full observer; horizontal position and yaw are therefore not scored.
PII provides a deterministic feedback-based heave reference, while OU--II is
the immediate stochastic predecessor of OU--III.

All four methods replay the same timestamped synthetic motion and inertial
records and are scored over the same final \SI{900}{s}.  The common comparison
is restricted to vertical displacement and tilt, which are available from every
method.  Parameters are frozen before evaluation and reference displacement is
never used to select gains.  OU--II and OU--III use the same wave-band period
input, so their comparison does not confound state architecture with the tuning
band.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Parameter and aiding policy for the deterministic baselines.}
\label{tab:baseline-tuning-policy}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{1.2cm}
                    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{2.4cm}
                    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{3.7cm}@{}}
\toprule
Method & Tuning policy & Aiding and scored outputs \\
\midrule
OU--III & committed adaptive configuration & IMU+mag; vertical and tilt scored \\
OU--II & committed predecessor configuration & same records and common outputs \\
PII & committed PLL scheduler & same records; common outputs only \\
TVG--NLO & fixed gains for vertical adaptation & IMU only; yaw/horizontal unscored \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\IfFileExists{w3d-baseline-results-generated.tex-part}{%
  \input{w3d-baseline-results-generated.tex-part}%
}{}

In the single-realization comparison, OU--III has the lowest mean normalized
vertical-displacement RMS error: \pct{4.5} of $H_s$, compared with \pct{6.5}
for OU--II, \pct{11.9} for PII, and \pct{14.5} for the adapted TVG--NLO.
These values provide a reproducible external reference but are not ensemble
estimates; the inferential OU-family statement comes from the paired ten-seed
study below.

The benchmark set has two important limitations.  Only OU--II is evaluated
under the full paired inferential protocol, so the strongest statistical claim
is an architectural comparison with the author's predecessor rather than a
ranking against published practice.  In addition, the adapted TVG--NLO is not a
reproduction of its GNSS- and compass-aided experiment, and the set does not
include a conventional frequency-domain double-integration heave estimator.

