\subsubsection{Ten-seed OU-family and adaptation study}
\IfFileExists{w3d-ou-validation-results-publication.tex-part}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-validation-results-publication.tex-part}%
}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-validation-results-generated.tex-part}%
}

\IfFileExists{ou_validation_vertical.svg}{%
  \begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=0.90\textwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_validation_vertical}
    \caption{Ten-seed normalized vertical-displacement RMS for the adaptive and
    fixed-tuning variants. Error bars are paired-bootstrap 95\% intervals.}
    \label{fig:ou_mc_vertical}
  \end{figure*}
}{}

Across the four stationary JONSWAP seas, the seed-wise mean normalized vertical
error is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III and
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II.  The paired OU--III-minus-OU--II difference is
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage point with bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$.
This paired difference is the primary effect-size statement; the ten paired
realizations establish repeatability within the stated simulator and sensor
model, not uncertainty over alternative simulators, vessels, spectra, or update
cadences.

\paragraph{Vertical versus three-dimensional displacement}
OU--III has lower vertical error in every stationary JONSWAP sea and in the
controlled transition.  The benefit is not a general three-dimensional
improvement: Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_axes} shows higher OU--III 3D displacement
error in \OUValidationThreeDHigherCount{} of the
\OUValidationThreeDScenarios{} scenarios, with the largest sea as the
exception.  The additional integral-state regularization should therefore be
interpreted primarily as a heave improvement; in most tested cases it incurs a
horizontal-displacement cost, especially in the $Y$ channel.

\paragraph{Second spectrum and direction}
The paired PM--Stokes ensemble reproduces the vertical advantage, with a
seed-level mean difference of $\OUValidationPMStokesDifference$ percentage
point
($[\OUValidationPMStokesDifferenceLow,\OUValidationPMStokesDifferenceHigh]$).
It is reported separately from the JONSWAP primary endpoint rather than pooled
with it.  Across the eight stationary seas, the wave-axis estimate has pooled
absolute circular-mean error \OUValidationDirectionAbsError{} degrees and no
scenario exceeds \OUValidationDirectionWorstAbsError{} degrees.  Truth-referenced
travel sense is correct for \OUValidationTravelCorrect\% of samples, wrong for
\OUValidationTravelWrong\%, and unresolved for
\OUValidationTravelUnresolved\%.  The exported FORWARD/BACKWARD names remain
vessel-frame labels and are not themselves treated as physical truth.

\paragraph{Adaptation mechanism}
Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_adaptation} shows that online tuning strongly reduces the
penalty of applying the single FixedNominal operating point to off-nominal seas.
At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ sea, where FixedNominal and FixedOracle
coincide by construction, OU--III Adaptive is slightly worse than the matched
fixed point by $\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalDifference$ percentage point
($[\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalLow,\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalHigh]$),
consistent with the cost of estimating the operating point from noisy signals.

The channel ablation identifies the source of the adaptive vertical benefit.
Keeping $r_S$ adaptive while freezing $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ closely tracks
the fully adaptive result, whereas adapting the OU parameters with $r_S$
frozen recovers essentially none of the off-nominal improvement
(Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_channels}).  For vertical reconstruction in these tests,
the evidence therefore supports \emph{adaptive integral-state regularization}
rather than a need for independent three-parameter OU adaptation.  The OU
parameters remain part of the prior and determine $r_S$ through the deployed
law; the ablation only shows that their direct adaptation contributes little to
the vertical score once the regularization channel is held fixed.

\paragraph{Controlled transition and covariance control}
The whole-window transition percentages are normalized by the final
$H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$ and are therefore descriptive.  The interval scores in
Table~\ref{tab:ou_transition_segments} provide the cleaner interpretation:
Adaptive matches the nominal fixed point in the pure-start interval and gains
as the sea changes and reaches the long-period endpoint.  Adaptive also beats
the endpoint-calibrated FixedOracle over the whole transition because that
reference is deliberately mismatched before the endpoint is reached.

The matched covariance-maintenance control changes normalized vertical error by
at most $\OUValidationCovSyncWorstDifference$ percentage point
($[\OUValidationCovSyncWorstLow,\OUValidationCovSyncWorstHigh]$) across the
reported matched comparisons.  Periodic re-alignment of the latent-acceleration
covariance is therefore not the source of the adaptation differences.  The
full covariance-control table is retained with the archived validation study
rather than repeated in the publication view.

